at/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fa0%2Ff5%2Fafe3c19947419c916d1921e4505f%2Fatsw-am-d12-250923-705.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4642+0+186/resize/900/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fa0%2Ff5%2Fafe3c19947419c916d1921e4505f%2Fatsw-am-d12-250923-705.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4642+0+186/resize/1200/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fa0%2Ff5%2Fafe3c19947419c916d1921e4505f%2Fatsw-am-d12-250923-705.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4642+0+186/resize/1600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fa0%2Ff5%2Fafe3c19947419c916d1921e4505f%2Fatsw-am-d12-250923-705.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4642+0+186/resize/1800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fa0%2Ff5%2Fafe3c19947419c916d1921e4505f%2Fatsw-am-d12-250923-705.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4642+0+186/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fa0%2Ff5%2Fafe3c19947419c916d1921e4505f%2Fatsw-am-d12-250923-705.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/jpeg" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4642+0+186/resize/1100/quality/50/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fa0%2Ff5%2Fafe3c19947419c916d1921e4505f%2Fatsw-am-d12-250923-705.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8256x4642+0+186/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fa0%2Ff5%2Fafe3c19947419c916d1921e4505f%2Fatsw-am-d12-250923-705.jpg" class="img" alt=" All That She Wants directed by Scarlett Bermingham and Andrew Rhymer" loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Of course&lt;/em&gt; Annie Murphy (Alexis on &lt;em&gt;Schitt's Creek&lt;/em&gt;)&lt;em&gt; &lt;/em&gt;would make for a great rom-com leading lady. Here she plays 30-something Emma, who's newly single, at a career crossroads, and desperately wants to become a mom. Enter Luke (Cooper Raiff), a charming grad student who's 10 years her junior and makes for a perfect one-night-stand, and … possibly more?&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;p&gt;I won't pretend this is breaking the genre's mold, but it doesn't need to; Murphy is unsurprisingly a hoot and has sweet chemistry with Raiff, while the familiar beats wrap around you like a cozy blanket. (This isn't explicitly a holiday movie, though several crucial scenes take place around the Christmas season. So it could easily become a perennial fave a la &lt;em&gt;When Harry Met Sally&lt;/em&gt;, one of its obvious cinematic forebears.)&lt;em&gt; &lt;/em&gt;As a bonus: Christine Baranksi, Bill Irwin, and Zosia Mamet co-star as Emma's mom, dad, and sister. As of this writing, it doesn't yet have a distributor.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;&lt;em&gt;Babies&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-143947" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 2168;         --source-height: 1219;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2168x1219+0+83/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F97%2F21%2F6203e42844dcb5dfc731aced5d30%2Fbabies-still-01.tiff 400w, https://npr.br